People don't struggle to become who they want to be and live the life they want to live, they struggle to be honest with themselves;

it's so much easier to blame a system than learning how to play better, it's so much easier to blame your teachers than figuring out how you learn best and start walking that path alone, it's so much easier to blame the food industry than taking responsibility for what you put in your mouth, it's so much easier to blame your parents, your government, capitalism, than to pick yourself up and taking responsibility for your current life; 

at the end of the day, there are quite a few common traits between all the people who started at the bottom and ended up wealthy and successful, it's the courage to be honest with themselves and face the painful reality, it's the intelligence to not victimize themselves regardless of how unfair and hopeless things seemingly are, and it's the childlike optimism and unwavering self-belief that they can learn anything and that they are actually just getting started.
